**3. Give sequences [-their introduction-]{+primitives that build them+} and [-elimination forms.** Six primitives: `length`, `at`,-]{+primitives that take them apart.** There are six. `length` gives the number of elements. `at` reads the element at a position.+} `tabulate` [-(build-]{+builds+} a sequence from a length and a function of [-position), `fold`, `scan`,-]{+position. `fold` reduces a sequence to one value, by applying a step function to each element in turn and carrying an accumulator. `scan` does the same+} and {+returns every intermediate value of the accumulator, as a sequence.+} `where` [-(the-]{+returns the+} positions [-satisfying-]{+that satisfy+} a [-predicate).-]{+predicate.+} Sequences become arrays, so `at` [-is-]{+costs+} one {+work+} unit instead of [-linear in-]{+a cost that grows linearly with+} the index.
