The same rule says what a *learned* container would be. On the store a container is a recipe for a space: which rows a value unfolds into, the map back, and how to rebuild or reduce. A library entry of that shape, a pair of terms that unfold and build and are inverse on the examples, would describe a data structure in the store's own nouns: a matrix as two nested position spaces, a tree as a position space with a parent endomap **[P]**. Nothing before Stage [-D-]{+D, the last of the four stages in which this plan is built (§11.3),+} depends on it.
@@ -19 +19 @@ The same rule says what a *learned* container would be. On the store a container
[-The-]{+Two published results are the+} evidence for granting this [-much: with-]{+much **[L]**. One synthesizer, λ², is given+} such combinators and [-deduction-]{+splits requirements+} through [-them, one published synthesizer-]{+them; it+} has a median {+time+} of 0.43 s on its benchmark [-suite; without them, rediscovering-]{+suite. A different system, DreamCoder, was not given them. It had to rediscover+} `fold` from a fixed-point [-combinator-]{+combinator, and that+} took about 7,700 CPU-hours [-**[L]**.-]{+(§4.1).+} Demanding that the kernel find lifting from raw recursion, in hours, on one thread, is not a realistic test of anything.
