Case 5 is research with replicated precedent, not [-engineering: published-]{+engineering. Published+} recursion synthesizers need examples {+that are+} closed under the recursive call, [-or-]{+meaning that the examples include the result of every recursive call the program makes. Otherwise they need+} angelic [-execution to relax-]{+execution, which assumes+} that {+a recursive call can return anything, synthesizes, and then checks+} **[L]**. {+Cases 1–4 are different.+} By inspection, [-cases 1–4-]{+they+} are the shapes of the functions in the current library **[P]**. [-The-]{+What has been run is narrower than that. On its own rows the+} prototype [-tested-]{+was run on+} first-order instances and {+on+} two[-function-argument-] forms [-on its own rows (§8.1), and-]{+that take a function argument (§8.1). On+} the kernel's [-specs for-]{+spec rows it was run on+} `foldl`, `seq-corr`, `best-shift`, [-`argmax-corr`,-]{+`argmax-corr`+} and [-`last-or` on theirs (§8.5).-]{+`last-or`, and not all of these were solved (§8.5 gives the outcome of each).+} The higher-order `map`, [-`foldr`,-]{+`foldr`+} and `filter` were not tested. `best-shift` plausibly needs two lifts, [-which-]{+and two lifts+} were not tested either.
